+## 0.3.3 (unreleased)
+
+Additive: new opt-in feature `pdfium`; without it the crate is unchanged. Orthic-Labs/rightkit#743 (ScrapeRight port). Not compiled or run when written; CI is the first compiler, and `Cargo.lock` needs the new `pdfium-render` entries and `rightkit-pdf` 0.3.3 (CI patch).
+
+- **`pdfium` feature (off by default): `pdfium::PdfiumEngine`.** `PdfiumEngine::load(&Path)` opens a PDFium dynamic library at an absolute path (relative paths are refused so a planted library cannot win the loader's search); `load_verified(&Path, sha256_hex)` checks the file's SHA-256 first. Then `page_count`, `page_text(bytes, index, password)`, `text_by_pages` and `render_page(bytes, index, dpi, password) -> PdfiumPageImage { width, height, rgba }` (dpi 1..=1200, refused over `MAX_PIXELS` = 60 million before PDFium allocates). Typed `PdfiumError` carries native messages as text, so `pdfium-render` is not part of the public API. One engine per process (PDFium's state is global): a second `load` is `AlreadyLoaded`, not a panic. No PDFium binary is linked or shipped and nothing is downloaded: the host provisions the library from `rightkit-ffmpeg`'s native catalog (`pdfium`, chromium/8021).
+- New optional dependency `pdfium-render` 0.9.3 (`default-features = false`, features `pdfium_latest`, `thread_safe`; no `image`). Unverified: `pdfium_latest` selects the 7881 API set while the catalog pins chromium/8021 (macOS rows `pinned`); the catalog's Windows row is still `planned` (ScrapeRight uses chromium/7920), so a live load on Windows is not proven. `tests/pdfium_live.rs` (ignored; set `RIGHTKIT_PDFIUM_LIBRARY`) is the check to run on each host.
